**Theorem 5.3 (The criterion's exact fixed-port and all-history meaning).**
Let $x\in\mathcal F$ be represented using (2.2), and define
$z_x=\max_{J\in\mathcal J_{15}}\|H_J(x)\|_{\rm op}$.
The finite port (2.9) has worst-case error $e(T_x)=z_x$, with the
suprema ranging over all original complex inputs and all actual
finite adaptive histories. Some pure
input, actual wait zero, one complete mode read and Stop attain
that error. If the common multipliers of Theorem 5.2 also give
$\beta-\tau^Ty=z_x$, they certify the original unrestricted
$\varepsilon_1=z_x$ and a legal attaining port.

Proof. Theorems 2.2 and 2.3 preserve the whole coefficient tuple and
give a legal finite port. Its adjoint residual at actual time $t$ is

$$
Q_J(t)-S_x^\dagger(Q_J(t))=O(t)^TH_J(x)O(t),
\tag{5.6}
$$

because $S_x$ is stationary. The norm is independent of $t$.
TP gives $S_x^\dagger(I)=I$, so complementary event residuals
are negatives and empty/full residuals vanish. The fifteen singleton
and pair events exhaust all event norms by complementation.

The actual-history step is exactly QMB's credited first-read argument.
Before the first read a deterministic policy either stops, with no
input-dependent record, or has waits summing to one nonnegative $t$.
For first result $s$, the two unnormalized post-read states are
$p_s\Pi_s$ and $p_s'\Pi_s$. The rest of the common finite protocol
defines a probability kernel $L_s$ with mass one, including its
controls and Stop. This kernel is defined by the state $\Pi_s$ even
when $p_s$ or $p_s'$ vanishes; no conditioning on a zero-probability
event is needed. With the first outcome retained, the two history
laws on its disjoint blocks are $p_sL_s$ and $p_s'L_s$, so their
TV equals $\tfrac12\sum_s|p_s-p_s'|$. Subsequent common
coarsening cannot increase it. Source-independent random control
mixes these comparisons under the same law, even when the random
control record is retained. This proves the upper for all finite
depths, not a bounded protocol family.

For arbitrary complex density inputs the event difference is the
expectation of the Hermitian residual in (5.6). Its largest absolute
expectation is its operator norm. Choose a maximizing $J$ at zero
and a real unit eigenvector $v$ of $H_J(x)$ with eigenvalue $\lambda$
satisfying $|\lambda|=z_x$. The allowed physical pure input
$Dvv^TD^\dagger$, followed by the actual wait-zero complete read
and Stop, has event difference $\lambda$. Its full-read TV is at
least $|\lambda|$ and at most the upper already proved, hence equals
$z_x$. If $z_x=0$, any pure input realizes equality. Grouping
the read's outcomes into $J$ is classical postprocessing, not a new
instrument. No inaccessible torus angle or limiting wait is needed.

Finally Theorem 5.2 supplies a lower on every original competitor,
while this one fixed legal port supplies the equal upper. This
proves the conditional matching statement. The all-history
correspondence itself is the credited QMB/QRC ingredient; the new
application is to the complete split generators and criterion (3.5).
$\square$

